XR500v dual FXS
===============

/etc/init.d/voip starts both lines automatically. Audio defaults match the
tested RAM setup: echo cancellation, mild noise reduction and 50% playback.
Use /etc/config/xr500v-voip for enable/disable and audio options, then restart
voip with handsets on-hook. Do not run the old laboratory voip-dual alongside.

Phone1: /dev/xr500v-voice, SIP LAN:5060, ctrl_tcp loopback:4444.
Phone2: /dev/xr500v-voice1, SIP LAN:5062, ctrl_tcp loopback:4445.
Each manager owns its ctrl_tcp connection. Diagnostics go through the root-only
FIFO under /var/run/xr500v-voip/phoneN/control; never displace it with nc.

Persistent configuration:
  /etc/xr500v-voip/config                 common baresip settings
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/accounts.template  @LANIP@ substitution
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/accounts        optional exact account-file override
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/config          optional per-line settings override
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/contacts        optional contacts override
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/speeddial       optional URI to dial on lifting

Accounts/config are rendered privately into /var/run/xr500v-voip/phoneN;
stored files are not overwritten. No test speed dial or ISP credentials are
shipped. Defaults are local SIP accounts phone1/phone2, regint=0 (no registrar).
Managed SIP accounts and pre-call keypad dialing are available through the
companion LuCI application. Local shortcuts are 1# and 2#, calling the other
physical port. Neither a successful local call nor a running SIP process
validates provider registration, in-call IVR support or emergency calling.

Legacy single-line accounts.template and contacts are retained for backup;
custom legacy account settings must be explicitly mapped to phoneN before
using the new dual service. Check real NAND configuration before sysupgrade.
The package's keep.d entry preserves the whole configuration directory.

The audio module statically links SpeexDSP; see speexdsp.COPYING for its notice.
This public snapshot is for integration review; retain your own known-good
firmware and configuration when testing.
